item.description:
arXiv:2611.14869v1 Announce Type: new 
Abstract: Talagrand's convolution conjecture, recently proven by J. Lu, S. Guo, and E. Fang, concerns the smoothing properties of the heat semigroup on $\{-1,1\}^N$. We follow their perturbed coupled reverse-heat approach to generalize the theorem to tensor powers of irreducible continuous-time Markov semigroups on finite state spaces. Specifically, let $(S_t)_{t\geq0}$ be the semigroup of an irreducible continuous-time Markov chain on a finite state space $G_0$ with stationary distribution $\pi$. Then, for every $\tau>0$, there exists a finite constant $C_{\tau,S,\pi}<\infty$ such that, for every $N\geq1$, every nonnegative function $f:G_0^N\to[0,\infty)$, and every $\eta>1$, \begin{align*}
  \pi^{\otimes N}\left(S_\tau^{\otimes N}f>\eta\int_{G_0^N}f\,d\pi^{\otimes N}\right)\leq\frac{C_{\tau,S,\pi}}{\eta\sqrt{\log\eta}}, \end{align*} where the constant is independent of $N$, $f$, and $\eta$. Most of the machinery developed by Lu, Guo, and Fang transfers cleanly, but since probability distributions on $|G_0|$ elements are not determined by their mean for $|G_0|>2$, we must use explicit terminal bridge laws to find a dimension-independent bound for the terminal-discrepancy estimate. We have formalized our result in Lean 4. The formalization is publicly available.
